--- body ---
## Context

Second half of the author's decision of 2026-11-10 on the documentary chain;
ticket 1502 carries the survey and the recognition side. Read it first.

At the moment a file is fetched, its identity is known and then dropped:

- `scripts/doifetch.sh --doi X` (harness cascade `scripts/fulltext_cascade.py`)
  prints `{"status", "source", "path", "error"}`. The DOI or ISBN it was
  given is not in that line. `skills/zotero/references/backfill.md:166-173`
  tells the agent to "record that source", naming no place. The file lands
  in `papers/` by default, while `rules/zotero.md` describes the chain as
  fetch, stage in `docs/`, archive in Zotero.
- `skills/zotero/scripts/probe-url.py` prints the URL, the final URL, the
  HTML meta and the identifiers it found, writes the file to `docs/<slug>`,
  and keeps nothing beside it.

After ticket 1502, a later run can still recognise a file by hash (if the
library holds it) or by an identifier printed in its pages. What no later
run can recover is the **URL of a document that has no other identifier**:
working papers, reports, HTML snapshots. That is the class the 1456 suffix
list was patching around (`GTAP-WP54`, `R60`, `WP480`), and a Zotero item's
`url` field is an exact key for it.

## Decision

- **One receipt shape** for both fetchers, printed as today's JSON line and
  carrying at least: `path`, `md5`, `identifiers` (`doi`, `isbn`, `arxiv`,
  `handle`, as given or found), `url` and `final_url` where there is one,
  `source`, `title`. Keys that exist today keep their names.
- **The receipt is also left in the staging folder**, keyed by the file's
  md5 so that a rename does not orphan it (sen-causal-reading renamed its
  whole `docs/` on 2026-10-20). One append-only file per staging folder;
  the name and format are the implementer's, inside the git-ignored folder.
  It is disposable project staging, not harness state: losing it degrades
  recognition to ticket 1502's order, nothing breaks.
- **Recognition reads it.** `check-staging` takes a receipt's identifiers
  and URL, ranked with the `.bib` entry's (given, not scraped), and
  `find-work --metadata` accepts the receipt as it accepts
  `extract-metadata` output, so the agent retypes nothing.
- **The DOI/ISBN fetcher stages into `docs/` by default**; `--output-dir`
  still wins.

Alternatives set aside: a sidecar per file (orphaned by renames, doubles the
folder); writing the identifier into the PDF (changes the bytes, so the hash
no longer equals a copy fetched elsewhere); a `.bib` entry at fetch time (a
fetched file is not yet a cited one, and the tracked `.bib` is the
project's).

## Out of scope

- Changing DOIfetch itself (another repository): the harness cascade owns
  the JSON line, so no upstream change is needed. A wish for upstream goes
  there as a ticket, not as an edit.
- A manifest of what a project cites (sen-causal-reading's `copies.csv`):
  that is the project's.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] `fulltext_cascade.py` and `probe-url.py` print the same receipt keys; a test pins the shared shape on both
- [ ] Each fetch leaves its receipt in the staging folder, keyed by md5; a test shows the receipt found after the file is renamed
- [ ] A file fetched by URL with no DOI, staged in one run, is recognised in a later run by its URL against an item's `url` field; a test shows it, with a control file that has no receipt
- [ ] `find-work --metadata <receipt>` works without hand editing; a test shows it
- [ ] `doifetch.sh --doi` with no `--output-dir` writes under `docs/`; `skills/zotero/references/backfill.md` says where the source is recorded instead of "record that source"
- [ ] `rules/zotero.md` states the handoff in one sentence (who writes the receipt, where), within the resident-rules budget
- [ ] `make check` passes
